WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.923
A temperature sensor feeds both the
controller

2
00:00:03.043 --> 00:00:05.043
and the high-temperature alarm.

3
00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:08.619
The diagram branches one signal into two
functions.

4
00:00:08.739 --> 00:00:12.643
Two boxes on the drawing do not make two
independent measurements.

5
00:00:12.763 --> 00:00:14.763
If the sensor fails low,

6
00:00:14.883 --> 00:00:18.104
both functions may receive the same
misleading value.

7
00:00:18.224 --> 00:00:22.401
Whether that matters depends on the
process risk and other protections.

8
00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:25.401
State the failure mechanism before
deciding

9
00:00:25.521 --> 00:00:30.043
whether an independent sensor or another
protective measure is necessary.

10
00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:34.994
The functional specification should
describe the response to a detected

11
00:00:35.114 --> 00:00:38.143
sensor disconnect, including control
behaviour

12
00:00:38.263 --> 00:00:41.933
and operator information appropriate to
the intended use.

13
00:00:42.053 --> 00:00:45.103
Our teaching example proposes inhibiting
heating

14
00:00:45.223 --> 00:00:48.573
and generating an identifiable alarm; it
is

15
00:00:48.693 --> 00:00:51.210
not a universal safe-state instruction.

16
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.762
Review whether that response introduces

17
00:00:53.882 --> 00:00:56.463
another hazard for the particular process.

18
00:00:56.583 --> 00:00:59.271
Distinguish an alarm that merely informs

19
00:00:59.391 --> 00:01:03.615
an operator from an interlock that changes
operation automatically.

20
00:01:04.235 --> 00:01:08.736
If the control system creates records used
for GMP decisions,

21
00:01:08.856 --> 00:01:13.571
identify those records and the applicable
computerized-system requirements.

22
00:01:13.691 --> 00:01:17.360
Consider who can change a setpoint, how
changes are controlled

23
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.275
and how necessary records remain
available.

24
00:01:20.395 --> 00:01:24.235
The European context is EU GMP Annex 11.

25
00:01:24.355 --> 00:01:30.691
Where relevant, also assess the
applicability of FDA 21 CFR Part 11.

26
00:01:30.811 --> 00:01:33.563
This module flags the interface; it does

27
00:01:33.683 --> 00:01:36.840
not complete computerized-system
validation

28
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.784
or demonstrate compliance with either
framework.

29
00:01:39.904 --> 00:01:41.973
A password screen by itself is

30
00:01:42.093 --> 00:01:44.696
not evidence of adequate access control,

31
00:01:44.816 --> 00:01:47.419
data integrity or record retention.

32
00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:51.217
Create a test intention for normal
operation,

33
00:01:51.337 --> 00:01:53.599
the identified fault and recovery.

34
00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:55.719
For our sensor scenario,

35
00:01:55.839 --> 00:01:59.337
a later test should challenge the fault in
a controlled way

36
00:01:59.457 --> 00:02:03.276
and verify the specified response and
operator indication.

37
00:02:03.396 --> 00:02:08.559
Also consider restoration: an uncontrolled
restart may be unacceptable.

38
00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:11.047
During DQ, check that the design

39
00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:13.855
and test method make the challenge
feasible.

40
00:02:13.975 --> 00:02:16.577
The actual execution, safety controls

41
00:02:16.697 --> 00:02:20.431
and acceptance decision belong to approved
site procedures.
